WEBVTT

00:00:01.390 --> 00:00:09.590
Hi, this is Dr. Jelena Trajkovic, CECS Department, Cal State Long Beach. Since we started working

00:00:09.590 --> 00:00:17.310
with sequential circuits, we will need to use clock signal. And let us look at how to

00:00:17.310 --> 00:00:29.280
implement a clock in Verilog. So clock is, let us remember, a periodical signal. And

00:00:29.280 --> 00:00:33.080
we defined duty cycle during our lecture time,

00:00:33.080 --> 00:00:38.080
but it is easiest to implement one with 50% duty cycles

00:00:38.280 --> 00:00:42.400
cycle in Verilog and that's what we are going to show here.

00:00:42.400 --> 00:00:47.280
So instead of manually coding that every,

00:00:47.280 --> 00:00:52.280
let's say 50 nanoseconds clock is going to become one

00:00:54.000 --> 00:00:56.200
and then after 50 nanoseconds

00:00:56.200 --> 00:00:58.800
it's going to become zero and so and so forth.

00:00:58.800 --> 00:01:02.400
For clock generator, we are going to use the code

00:01:02.400 --> 00:01:06.040
that is here shown in line 16 and 17

00:01:06.040 --> 00:01:08.640
on the right hand side of the screen.

00:01:08.640 --> 00:01:11.720
It is very important to remember

00:01:11.720 --> 00:01:16.720
that we have to place this code inside of a test bench,

00:01:17.420 --> 00:01:20.000
but outside of the initial statement.

00:01:21.160 --> 00:01:24.120
However, inside of initial statement,

00:01:24.120 --> 00:01:27.720
we need to place the first initial value

00:01:27.720 --> 00:01:31.240
of the clock cycle, clock variable.

00:01:31.240 --> 00:01:34.680
So here it's going to be CLK underscore TB,

00:01:34.680 --> 00:01:39.680
and we are going to choose that it becomes a zero

00:01:39.940 --> 00:01:43.940
in the moment the initialization is invoked.

00:01:45.240 --> 00:01:51.430
This always statement is going to basically

00:01:52.510 --> 00:01:56.590
assign new value, which is equals to naught

00:01:56.590 --> 00:02:01.410
of current value, therefore this becomes one,

00:02:01.410 --> 00:02:05.610
after 50 nanoseconds to the clock TV variable.

00:02:07.270 --> 00:02:11.170
Again, in following 50 nanoseconds,

00:02:11.170 --> 00:02:15.030
this is going to change the value and so on and so forth.

00:02:15.030 --> 00:02:20.030
The simulation result is going to look similar

00:02:20.630 --> 00:02:23.350
to the one shown here on the bottom.

00:02:23.350 --> 00:02:26.130
And should you have decided to start

00:02:26.130 --> 00:02:29.450
with clock underscore tv equals to one,

00:02:29.450 --> 00:02:33.490
the waveform is going to be flipped.

00:02:33.490 --> 00:02:36.330
However, in this particular example,

00:02:36.330 --> 00:02:39.650
we started with clock tv equals to zero

00:02:39.650 --> 00:02:42.690
and then for first 15 nanosecond it was zero,

00:02:42.690 --> 00:02:45.610
then one, and so on and so forth.

00:02:45.610 --> 00:02:47.130
Hope this is helpful.

00:02:47.130 --> 00:02:47.970
Thank you.

